export const R6={title:'Non-sterile compounding, equipment and oral suspensions (Skills I and IV)',hook:'A three-year-old needs a medicine that is made only as a large adult tablet. A pharmacy can crush the tablets and suspend the powder in a flavored liquid at a child-sized strength. That is compounding. This lesson covers the standards for non-sterile compounding, the instruments you must identify in Skill I and the 18 steps of compounding an oral suspension in Skill IV.',objectives:['Explain when compounding is appropriate and the role of USP <795>.','Identify pharmacy instruments and equipment and their uses (Skill I).','Calculate the number of tablets for a suspension from a Master Formula.','Compound, package and label an oral suspension following HOSA Skill IV.'],sections:[
['What compounding is',[
'**Compounding** is preparing a customized medication for an individual patient when a commercial product does not meet the need—for example, a liquid for a child who cannot swallow tablets, a dye-free product for an allergic patient or a strength that is not manufactured. **Non-sterile compounding** (oral liquids, creams, ointments, capsules, suppositories, troches) follows **USP <795>**. **Sterile compounding** (injections, IV bags, eye drops) follows **USP <797>**, and handling **hazardous drugs** follows **USP <800>**.',
'Every compounded preparation is made from a **Master Formulation Record** (HOSA calls it the **Master Formula sheet**) that lists ingredients, quantities, equipment, steps, the **beyond-use date (BUD)** and storage. Each batch is documented on a **Compounding Record** with lot numbers and expiration dates of ingredients, who prepared it and who checked it. USP <795> sets default BUDs—for example, **14 days refrigerated** for a non-preserved aqueous preparation and **35 days** for a preserved aqueous preparation—unless stability data support a different date.',
sort('Sterile or non-sterile compounding?',['Non-sterile (USP <795>)','Sterile (USP <797>)'],[['Oral suspension',0],['IV antibiotic bag',1],['Topical cream',0],['Eye drops',1],['Rectal suppository',0],['Syringe of insulin for injection',1]])]],
['Instruments and equipment (Skill I)',[
'In **Skill I** you see 15 numbered photos or items and write each one’s **name (spelled correctly)** and **purpose**—45 points in 15 minutes. Non-sterile tools include the **mortar and pestle** (glass for liquids and staining drugs; porcelain or Wedgwood for grinding powders), **spatula** (mixing and transferring), **compounding slab** (ointment mixing surface), **conical graduates** and **graduated cylinders** (measuring liquids—cylinders are more accurate), **beakers**, **stirring rods**, **pipettes** and **medicine droppers**, **weights** and **counting trays**.',
'Other items on the list: **suppository molds** (aluminum and plastic), **tablet mold**, **heat gun** and **hot plate**, **tongs**, **crimper** (seals vial caps), **ampules**, **syringes** (insulin, tuberculin, IV safety syringes), **liquid oral syringe**, **calibrated spoon**, **medicine cup**, **transdermal patch**, **tissue forceps** and **hemostatic forceps**, **autoclave** (steam sterilizer) and **indicator strip** (shows sterilization occurred), **ultrasonic cleaning unit**, **laminar airflow hood**, **sharps container**, **eyewash station** and **auxiliary labels**.',
match('Name the purpose',[['Mortar and pestle','Crushing and mixing powders'],['Compounding slab','Smooth surface for mixing ointments'],['Graduated cylinder','Measuring liquid volume accurately'],['Crimper','Sealing caps on vials'],['Tuberculin syringe','Measuring small volumes up to 1 mL'],['Insulin syringe','Measuring insulin in units'],['Indicator strip','Shows that sterilization conditions were met'],['Laminar airflow hood','Filtered airflow for sterile compounding']]),
flip('Easily confused items',[['Conical graduate vs. cylinder','Cylinder has straight sides and is more accurate'],['Glass vs. porcelain mortar','Glass for liquids and staining drugs; porcelain for grinding'],['Tissue vs. hemostatic forceps','Tissue forceps grasp; hemostats clamp'],['Autoclave','Steam sterilizer'],['Ultrasonic cleaner','Sound waves clean instruments'],['Heat gun','Directs heat, e.g., to smooth suppositories']])]],
['Planning a suspension',[
'A **suspension** has fine drug particles spread through a liquid **vehicle** that slows settling, such as a commercial suspending vehicle mixed with a flavored syrup. Before compounding, obtain the completed **Master Formula sheet** from the scenario and **check the amount per dose and the total volume** with the pharmacist. Then calculate how many tablets you need: **total drug needed = concentration × final volume**, and **tablets = total drug ÷ strength per tablet**.',
'Finished suspensions in the event are **60 mL or greater**. Accuracy matters because the judge awards points only if you obtain the correct number of tablets. If the calculation gives a fraction of a tablet, the Master Formula usually directs you to round up and adjust, or the pharmacist will decide—never guess.',
{t:'example',title:'How many tablets?',prompt:'The Master Formula calls for 60 mL of a 25 mg/mL suspension using 250 mg tablets. (Invented for practice.)',steps:['Total drug: 25 mg/mL × 60 mL = 1,500 mg','Tablets: 1,500 mg ÷ 250 mg/tablet = 6 tablets'],answer:'Obtain 6 tablets and verify the drug and count with the pharmacist.'},
chk('A formula requires 100 mL of a 10 mg/mL suspension from 500 mg tablets. How many tablets?',['2','5','10','20'],'10 × 100 = 1,000 mg; 1,000 ÷ 500 = 2 tablets.')]],
['Compounding the suspension: Skill IV',[
'Skill IV has 18 steps in 15 minutes. **1.** Obtain the Master Formula. **2.** Check the dose and total volume with the pharmacist. **3.** Obtain the correct number of tablets and **verify the product and count** with the pharmacist. **4.** Obtain equipment. **5.** Hand hygiene. **6.** Don **hair cover, face mask, gloves and gown**. **7.** **Crush** the tablets to a **fine powder** with the mortar and pestle. **8.** Add a small amount of vehicle to **wet the powder** and **levigate**—mix until a **smooth paste** forms. **9.** Keep adding vehicle until the mixture can be poured.',
'**10.** Pour into a **graduated cylinder**. **11.** **Rinse the mortar** several times with small amounts of vehicle and add the rinses to the cylinder so no drug is lost. **12.** Add vehicle to the **final volume** and read it **at eye level** at the bottom of the meniscus. **13.** Pour into the best-sized **amber oval bottle**, seal and **shake well**. **14.** Affix the correct prescription label. **15.** Affix the correct **auxiliary labels** (such as “Shake well” and “Refrigerate” if required). **16.** Clean the area and equipment with **70% isopropyl alcohol**. **17.** Remove PPE. **18.** Hand hygiene.',
order('Skill IV sequence',['Check the Master Formula, dose and volume with the pharmacist','Obtain and verify tablets; obtain equipment','Hand hygiene and PPE','Crush to a fine powder','Levigate into a smooth paste','Add vehicle until pourable and transfer to a cylinder','Rinse the mortar into the cylinder','Bring to final volume at eye level','Bottle, shake and label with auxiliary labels','Clean with 70% IPA, remove PPE, hand hygiene'])]],
['Quality and safety in compounding',[
'Good compounding practice means **clean equipment**, a **clean, uncluttered area**, accurate **weighing and measuring**, and **documentation** of every step. Read volumes at **eye level**; reading from above or below gives parallax error. **Levigation** prevents gritty lumps that can cause uneven doses. Amber bottles protect light-sensitive drugs, and oval bottles fit oral syringes and are easy to hold. Labels must show the BUD and storage conditions.',
'Use PPE to protect yourself and the product, and handle hazardous drugs only with USP <800> precautions. If a powder becomes airborne or a spill occurs, follow the spill procedure and the Safety Data Sheet. Report any discrepancy—a missing ingredient, an unexpected color or a calculation that does not check out—to the pharmacist before dispensing.',
kase('Compounding check',{a:{text:'After adding vehicle to the 60 mL line, you notice you forgot to rinse the mortar. What is the problem?',choices:[['Some drug remains in the mortar, so the suspension may be too weak','b','Correct. Rinsing transfers all the drug.',true],['There is no problem','b','Drug left behind lowers the concentration.',false]]},b:{text:'What should you do?',choices:[['Tell the pharmacist, who decides whether to remake it','c','Correct. Report discrepancies before dispensing.',true],['Add more tablets without telling anyone','c','Undocumented changes are unsafe.',false]]},c:{text:'The pharmacist has you remake the preparation, rinsing the mortar before adjusting to final volume. Well done for reporting it.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Compounding customizes medicines; USP <795> (non-sterile), <797> (sterile) and <800> (hazardous); every preparation follows a Master Formula with a beyond-use date.','Skill I: name, spell and give the purpose of 15 items such as mortar and pestle, slab, cylinder, crimper, tuberculin and insulin syringes, autoclave and laminar airflow hood.','Skill IV: verify formula and tablets, PPE, crush, levigate, transfer, rinse the mortar, bring to volume at eye level, amber bottle, shake, label, auxiliary labels, clean with 70% IPA.'],terms:[['Levigation','Wetting and mixing a powder into a smooth paste before adding more liquid.'],['Beyond-use date','The date after which a compounded preparation should not be used.'],['Master Formulation Record','The recipe and instructions for a compounded preparation.']],mcqs:[q('Which USP chapter covers non-sterile compounding?','USP <795>','USP <797>','USP <800>','USP <71>','<797> is sterile; <800> hazardous.'),q('What is levigation?','Mixing a powder with a small amount of liquid into a smooth paste','Measuring a liquid at eye level','Sealing a vial cap','Heating a suppository mold','Prevents gritty lumps.'),q('Why rinse the mortar into the graduated cylinder?','To transfer all of the drug','To cool the mortar','To dilute the label','It is not needed','Otherwise the product is too weak.'),q('How many 200 mg tablets are needed for 80 mL of a 10 mg/mL suspension?','4','8','2','10','10 × 80 = 800 mg ÷ 200.'),q('Which bottle is used for the finished suspension in Skill IV?','Amber oval bottle','Clear glass vial','Ampule','Plastic bag','Light protection and easy pouring.'),q('Which instrument seals caps on vials?','Crimper','Spatula','Tongs','Pipette','Crimpers seal aluminum caps.'),q('What does an indicator strip show?','Sterilization conditions were met','The drug’s expiration date','The weight of a powder','The pH of a solution','Used with autoclaves.'),q('How should the final volume be read?','At eye level at the bottom of the meniscus','From above','From below','By weight only','Avoids parallax error.')],frq:{prompt:'Using a Master Formula that calls for 120 mL of a 5 mg/mL oral suspension from 100 mg tablets, explain your calculation and describe how you would compound, package and label the suspension following Skill IV. (Invented for practice.)',rubric:[['Calculation',['600','6 tablets','5 mg/ml','120','verify']],['Preparation',['hand','ppe','hair cover','mask','gloves','gown','crush','fine powder']],['Compounding technique',['levigate','paste','vehicle','graduated cylinder','rinse','eye level','final volume']],['Packaging and cleanup',['amber','shake','label','auxiliary','70%','isopropyl','remove ppe']]],model:'The total drug needed is 5 mg/mL × 120 mL = 600 mg, and 600 mg ÷ 100 mg per tablet = 6 tablets. I check the dose and total volume with the pharmacist, obtain 6 tablets and verify the product and count with the pharmacist, gather the mortar and pestle, graduated cylinder and suspending vehicle, perform hand hygiene and put on a hair cover, face mask, gloves and gown. I crush the tablets to a fine powder, add a small amount of vehicle and levigate until a smooth paste forms, then add more vehicle until it can be poured. I pour it into a graduated cylinder, rinse the mortar several times with small amounts of vehicle and add the rinses so no drug is lost, and bring it to 120 mL, reading at eye level at the bottom of the meniscus. I pour it into an appropriately sized amber oval bottle, seal it and shake it well, then affix the correct prescription label with the beyond-use date and auxiliary labels such as “Shake well” and “Refrigerate” if required. I clean the area and equipment with 70% isopropyl alcohol, return equipment, remove my PPE and perform hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,USP_COMP,FDA_COMP]};

export const R7={title:'Sterile compounding, aseptic garbing and withdrawing from a vial (Skills III and VII)',hook:'A single bacterium injected into a patient’s bloodstream can cause a deadly infection. In 2012, contaminated steroid injections from one compounding pharmacy caused a nationwide fungal meningitis outbreak that killed more than 60 people. Sterile compounding rules exist to make sure that never happens again. Skills VII and III test garbing, hand washing, gloving and aseptic technique.',objectives:['Explain the clean-room design and primary engineering controls in USP <797>.','Garb, scrub and glove in the correct order following HOSA Skill VII.','Clean a laminar airflow hood and use first air correctly.','Withdraw liquid from a vial using aseptic technique following HOSA Skill III.'],sections:[
['Clean rooms and hoods',[
'Sterile preparations are made in a **primary engineering control (PEC)** such as a **laminar airflow workbench (LAFW)** or a **biological safety cabinet (BSC)** for hazardous drugs. The PEC provides **ISO Class 5** air—filtered through a **HEPA filter** that removes at least 99.97% of particles 0.3 microns in size. The PEC sits in a **buffer room** (ISO Class 7), entered through an **anteroom** (ISO Class 8 or better), where staff wash and garb. Air pressure is designed so air flows from cleaner areas to less clean areas.',
'**First air** is the HEPA-filtered air coming directly from the filter before it touches anything. Nothing should come between the filter and the **critical sites**—the needle, the vial stopper and the syringe tip. Work at least **6 inches inside** the hood; HOSA’s Skill III rating sheet places supplies in the **outer six-inch staging area** and the vial in the **Direct Compounding Area (DCA)**. Do not cough, talk over the work or block airflow with your hands.',
order('From least clean to cleanest',['General pharmacy area','Anteroom (ISO 8): hand washing and garbing','Buffer room (ISO 7)','Hood / PEC (ISO 5): first air at the critical site'])]],
['Garbing order: Skill VII steps 1–8',[
'Skill VII has 32 steps in 10 minutes. Garbing goes from **dirtiest to cleanest**. **1.** Remove outer garments and **jewelry**. **2.** Obtain supplies. **3.** Cleanse hands with **sterile, foamed 70% IPA**, holding the can with the tip down into the palm and coating palms, backs and each finger; allow to dry. **4.** Don **shoe covers** one at a time, covering each shoe completely. **5.** Reapply foamed IPA. **6.** Tie back hair and put on the **hair cover**, covering all hair. **7.** Reapply foamed IPA. **8.** Don the **face mask** over the nose, mouth and chin, and a **beard cover** if needed.',
'The repeated alcohol foam between steps keeps your hands as clean as possible as you touch each new item. Long hair is tied back so it fits completely under the cover. Remember the overall order: **shoe covers → hair cover → mask (beard cover) → hand scrub → gown → foam → sterile gloves**.',
order('Garbing sequence',['Remove jewelry and outer garments','Foam hands with 70% IPA','Shoe covers','Foam; hair cover','Foam; face mask (beard cover if needed)','Aseptic hand and forearm scrub','Sterile gown','Foam with IPA; sterile gloves'])]],
['Aseptic hand washing: steps 9–24',[
'At the sink, squeeze the **surgical scrub sponge/brush** to activate the soap, open the packet and hold the sponge in your **dominant hand**, discarding the wrapper with the other hand. Start the water with the **foot pedals** (or ask the judge) and wet your hands when it is warm. Use the **nail pick** under each fingernail and discard it. Add a little water to the sponge. Use the **brush side** under the nails of the **left hand from thumb to pinkie**, then the right.',
'Use the **sponge side** to clean the **four surfaces** of each finger (top, side, bottom, side) and the webbing, beginning with the thumb, on the left hand and then the right. Clean the **palm** of each hand, then each **forearm in a circular pattern from wrist to elbow**. Discard the sponge without touching the waste container. **Rinse** each hand and forearm with **fingers pointed up**, from fingers to elbow, so water drains away from the clean hands. **Dry** with an aseptic, **lint-free** towel from **fingers toward the elbow**, then discard it.',
order('Scrub sequence for each hand',['Nail pick under each nail','Brush under nails, thumb to pinkie','Sponge four surfaces of each finger and webbing','Sponge the palm','Forearm in circles, wrist to elbow','Rinse fingers up, fingers to elbow','Dry with lint-free towel, fingers to elbow'])]],
['Gown, gloves and removal: steps 25–32',[
'**25.** Open the sterile gown. **26.** Put one arm into a sleeve and pull it up onto the shoulder, then the other; secure the gown at the **neck and waist**. **27.** Sanitize hands with **sterile foamed 70% IPA**. **28.** Open the sterile gloves on a clean surface. **29.** Put the **left glove** on the left hand by grasping the **inner part of the cuff** with the right hand and pulling it on. **30.** Repeat on the right hand. Touch only the inside of the cuff with bare skin so the outside stays sterile.',
'When the judge says the skill is complete, **remove PPE**: grasp the cuff of one glove and pull it off; while holding it in the gloved hand, grasp the cuff of the other glove and pull it off over the first; discard the bundle. Untie the gown and remove it by pulling at the shoulders, **turning it inside out**, and discard it. Remove the **face mask, hair cover and shoe covers** and discard them. **32.** Perform hand hygiene.',
flip('Skill VII details',[['Foamed IPA','Tip down into the palm; coat all surfaces; let dry'],['Rinse direction','Fingers up, from fingers to elbow'],['Drying','Lint-free towel, fingers toward elbow'],['Gown','Arms in, secure at neck and waist'],['Gloves','Grasp inner cuff; left first in this sheet'],['Removal','Gloves, gown inside out, mask, hair cover, shoe covers, hand hygiene']])]],
['Cleaning the hood and handling syringes',[
'Before compounding, clean the hood with **sterile 70% isopropyl alcohol** and lint-free wipes, working **from the cleanest area outward**: the **IV bar/hooks** first, then the **sides from back to front and top to bottom**, then the **work surface from back to front** in overlapping strokes. **Never spray the HEPA filter.** In Skill III you must verbalize which surfaces you cleaned and with what. Swab vial stoppers with alcohol and let them dry before entering.',
'A **syringe** has a **barrel** with measurement marks, a **plunger** and a **tip** (often **Luer-lock**). A **needle** has a **hub**, a **shaft** and a **bevel** (the slanted tip). The **gauge** describes the needle’s diameter: **the larger the number, the thinner the needle**. Never touch the plunger’s shaft, the syringe tip or the needle—these are critical sites. Choose the **smallest syringe that holds the volume** for the best accuracy.',
match('Syringe and needle parts',[['Barrel','Holds the fluid; has calibration marks'],['Plunger','Pushes or pulls fluid'],['Luer-lock tip','Twists to lock the needle on'],['Hub','Part of the needle that attaches to the syringe'],['Bevel','Slanted tip of the needle'],['Gauge','Needle diameter; higher number is thinner']])]],
['Withdrawing from a vial: Skill III',[
'Skill III has 28 steps in 15 minutes. You obtain the order and supplies, **calculate the volume** from the scenario and verbalize that you have donned PPE, washed hands and donned sterile gloves. After cleaning the hood, place supplies in the **outer six-inch staging area** and the vial in the **DCA**. Remove the flip-top cap, swab the stopper and discard swabs and wrappers in the discard pile. Attach the needle to the syringe, **pull the plunger back to slightly less than the volume needed** and place the needle cap on an alcohol swab.',
'Stabilize the vial with the thumb and forefinger of your nondominant hand, insert the needle **bevel up at a 45-degree angle** through the stopper and **inject the air** into the vial—this equalizes pressure so fluid flows out easily. **Invert** the vial so the needle is below it, let fluid flow into the syringe and pull back to the exact volume. **Tap out bubbles** toward the hub and push them back into the vial. Withdraw the needle, carefully recap, **label** the syringe, place it on the transport vehicle, discard waste properly, return unused supplies, verbalize removing PPE in the anteroom and perform hand hygiene.',
order('Skill III core technique',['Swab the vial stopper','Attach the needle; draw air slightly less than the volume needed','Insert bevel up at 45° and inject the air','Invert the vial and withdraw the volume','Tap bubbles to the hub and push them into the vial','Withdraw and recap; label the syringe']),
{t:'w',kind:'dosecalc',title:'Volume to withdraw',types:['liquid'],prompt:'Calculate the volume to draw up (desired ÷ have × volume) before you touch the vial.'},
kase('Aseptic technique',{a:{text:'While working, you reach across the vial between the HEPA filter and the needle. What happened?',choices:[['You blocked first air to a critical site—discard and start again as policy requires','b','Correct. Blocking first air risks contamination.',true],['Nothing; the hood filters everything','b','Your hand can shed particles onto the needle.',false]]},b:{text:'You see large bubbles in the syringe. What do you do?',choices:[['Tap them toward the hub and push them back into the vial, then recheck the volume','c','Correct. Bubbles make the measured volume inaccurate.',true],['Ignore them','c','Bubbles take up space meant for drug.',false]]},c:{text:'You recap, label the syringe and place it on the transport vehicle. Skill complete.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Sterile compounding follows USP <797>: ISO 5 hood with HEPA first air inside an ISO 7 buffer room and ISO 8 anteroom; never block first air to critical sites; work 6 inches in.','Skill VII: jewelry off, foam, shoe covers, foam, hair cover, foam, mask, nail pick and scrub (thumb to pinkie, four surfaces, palm, forearm wrist to elbow), rinse fingers up, dry, gown, foam, gloves; remove in reverse.','Skill III: clean the hood, stage supplies, swab the stopper, inject equal air bevel up at 45°, invert and withdraw, remove bubbles, recap, label and discard waste correctly.'],terms:[['First air','HEPA-filtered air coming directly from the filter before it touches anything.'],['Critical site','A surface such as a needle or vial stopper that must remain sterile.'],['Aseptic technique','Practices that prevent contamination of sterile products.']],mcqs:[q('What ISO class is the air inside a laminar airflow workbench?','ISO Class 5','ISO Class 7','ISO Class 8','ISO Class 9','The buffer room is ISO 7.'),q('Which garb is put on first in Skill VII?','Shoe covers','Sterile gloves','Gown','Face mask','Dirtiest to cleanest.'),q('How should hands be held while rinsing after the aseptic scrub?','Fingers pointed up','Fingers pointed down','Flat','It does not matter','Water drains away from the hands toward the elbows.'),q('Which part of the hood is never sprayed?','The HEPA filter','The work surface','The IV bar','The side walls','Spraying can damage the filter.'),q('Why inject air into a vial before withdrawing fluid?','To equalize pressure so fluid withdraws easily','To mix the drug','To sterilize the needle','To check the needle','Prevents a vacuum.'),q('At what angle is the needle inserted into the stopper in Skill III?','45 degrees, bevel up','90 degrees, bevel down','10 degrees','180 degrees','Per the rating sheet.'),q('A 25-gauge needle compared with an 18-gauge needle is:','Thinner','Thicker','The same','Longer','Higher gauge = smaller diameter.'),q('How far inside the hood should work be done?','At least 6 inches','1 inch','At the very edge','Outside the hood','Supplies are staged in the outer six inches.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the order and technique for aseptic garbing, hand washing and gloving in Skill VII, and explain why each part of the order matters.',rubric:[['Preparation and garb',['jewelry','foam','ipa','shoe covers','hair cover','mask','beard']],['Hand scrub',['nail pick','thumb to pinkie','four surfaces','palm','forearm','wrist to elbow','fingers up','lint-free']],['Gown and gloves',['gown','neck','waist','foam','inner cuff','sterile gloves']],['Reasoning and removal',['dirtiest','cleanest','contamination','inside out','hand hygiene']]],model:'I remove jewelry and outer garments, gather supplies and cleanse my hands with sterile foamed 70% IPA, coating every surface and letting it dry. I put on shoe covers one at a time, foam again, tie back my hair and put on a hair cover that covers all hair, foam again and put on a face mask over my nose, mouth and chin, with a beard cover if needed. This dirtiest-to-cleanest order means the items closest to the floor are handled first, and the repeated foam keeps my hands clean as I touch each item. At the sink I activate the scrub sponge, start the water with the foot pedals, clean under each nail with the nail pick, brush under the nails from thumb to pinkie on each hand, sponge the four surfaces and webbing of each finger, the palm and each forearm in circles from wrist to elbow, discard the sponge without touching the bin, rinse with fingers pointed up from fingers to elbow so water runs away from the clean hands and dry with a lint-free towel from fingers to elbow. I put on the sterile gown, secure it at the neck and waist, foam my hands again and put on sterile gloves by touching only the inner cuff. To remove, I take off the gloves, then the gown inside out, then the mask, hair cover and shoe covers, and perform hand hygiene.'},sources:[RULES,USP_COMP,FDA_COMP,CDC_SHARPS]};

export const R8={title:'Disorders and treatments I: cardiovascular and respiratory drugs',hook:'Heart disease is the leading cause of death in the United States, and high blood pressure affects nearly half of American adults. Asthma and COPD affect millions more. That means cardiovascular and respiratory drugs fill a huge share of prescriptions. Learning the drug classes, name stems and key counseling points makes nearly half of the test far easier.',objectives:['Describe hypertension, hyperlipidemia, heart failure, angina and clotting disorders.','Match cardiovascular drug classes to examples, stems and key side effects.','Describe asthma and COPD and their rescue and controller medicines.','Recognize important counseling points technicians should refer to the pharmacist.'],sections:[
['Hypertension',[
'**Hypertension** is blood pressure that stays at or above **130/80 mm Hg** (AHA/ACC definition). It damages arteries and raises the risk of stroke, heart attack, kidney disease and heart failure, often without symptoms. Treatment starts with lifestyle changes—less salt, more activity, weight loss and limiting alcohol—and adds medicines when needed.',
'Major classes: **thiazide diuretics** (hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone) remove salt and water; **ACE inhibitors** (**-pril**: lisinopril, enalapril) relax vessels but can cause a **dry cough** and rarely **angioedema**; **ARBs** (**-sartan**: losartan, valsartan) work similarly without the cough; **calcium channel blockers** (**-dipine**: amlodipine; also diltiazem, verapamil) relax vessels and may cause **ankle swelling**; **beta blockers** (**-olol**: metoprolol, atenolol, carvedilol) slow the heart and **should not be stopped suddenly**. ACE inhibitors and ARBs are not used in **pregnancy**.',
match('Match the stem to the class',[['-pril','ACE inhibitor'],['-sartan','Angiotensin II receptor blocker'],['-olol','Beta blocker'],['-dipine','Calcium channel blocker'],['-thiazide','Thiazide diuretic'],['-statin','HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor']]),
label('heart','Where cardiovascular drugs act',[['lv','Left ventricle','Beta blockers reduce its workload; heart failure weakens it.'],['aorta','Aorta','High pressure here strains the heart.'],['ra','Right atrium','Contains the SA node; atrial fibrillation begins in the atria.'],['la','Left atrium','Clots can form here in atrial fibrillation.']])]],
['Cholesterol, angina and heart failure',[
'**Hyperlipidemia** (high LDL cholesterol) builds plaque in arteries. **Statins** (atorvastatin/Lipitor, rosuvastatin/Crestor, simvastatin) lower LDL; report **muscle pain**, and avoid large amounts of **grapefruit juice** with some statins. Other options include ezetimibe and PCSK9 inhibitors (alirocumab, evolocumab). **Angina** is chest pain from reduced blood flow to the heart. **Nitroglycerin sublingual** tablets or spray relieve attacks; patients typically take one dose at onset, and if pain is not improved or worsens **5 minutes** after the first dose, they **call 911**. Nitroglycerin tablets are kept in their **original glass bottle** away from heat and moisture, and must not be combined with erectile dysfunction drugs such as sildenafil.',
'**Heart failure** means the heart cannot pump enough blood. Treatments include **loop diuretics** such as **furosemide** (Lasix) to remove fluid—watch for **low potassium** and dehydration—ACE inhibitors or ARBs or **sacubitril/valsartan**, certain beta blockers, **spironolactone** (potassium-sparing; can raise potassium) and **SGLT2 inhibitors** such as empagliflozin. **Digoxin** strengthens contractions and has a **narrow therapeutic index**; nausea or vision changes may signal toxicity.',
sort('Common side effect',['Dry cough','Ankle swelling','Muscle pain','Low potassium'],[['Lisinopril',0],['Amlodipine',1],['Atorvastatin',2],['Furosemide',3],['Enalapril',0],['Nifedipine',1]])]],
['Clots, anticoagulants and antiplatelets',[
'Blood clots can cause **deep vein thrombosis (DVT)**, **pulmonary embolism** and **stroke**, especially with **atrial fibrillation**. **Anticoagulants** slow clot formation: **warfarin** (Jantoven) requires regular **INR** blood tests and consistent **vitamin K** intake (green leafy vegetables) and has many interactions; **direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs)** such as **apixaban** (Eliquis), **rivaroxaban** (Xarelto) and **dabigatran** (Pradaxa) need less monitoring; **heparin** and **enoxaparin** (Lovenox) are injected.',
'**Antiplatelet** drugs keep platelets from clumping: **aspirin**, **clopidogrel** (Plavix) and **ticagrelor** (Brilinta), often after a heart attack or stent. All of these raise **bleeding** risk. Patients should report black stools, blood in urine, unusual bruising or nosebleeds and tell every provider and dentist they take them. NSAIDs such as ibuprofen add to bleeding risk, so OTC questions go to the pharmacist.',
sort('Anticoagulant or antiplatelet?',['Anticoagulant','Antiplatelet'],[['Warfarin',0],['Clopidogrel',1],['Apixaban',0],['Aspirin',1],['Enoxaparin',0],['Ticagrelor',1]])]],
['Asthma',[
'**Asthma** is chronic inflammation and narrowing of the airways, with wheezing, cough, chest tightness and shortness of breath triggered by allergens, exercise, cold air, smoke or infections. Medicines fall into two groups. **Quick-relief (rescue)** inhalers—**short-acting beta₂ agonists (SABA)** such as **albuterol** (ProAir, Ventolin)—open the airways within minutes. **Long-term controllers** reduce inflammation: **inhaled corticosteroids (ICS)** such as **fluticasone** and **budesonide** (patients **rinse the mouth** afterward to prevent thrush), and **leukotriene modifiers** such as **montelukast** (Singulair), which carries an FDA boxed warning about mood and behavior changes.',
'**Long-acting beta₂ agonists (LABA)** such as **salmeterol** and **formoterol** are used **with an ICS**, not alone, in asthma (Advair, Symbicort). Current guidelines also allow certain ICS-formoterol inhalers as both controller and reliever. Severe asthma may require **oral corticosteroids** (prednisone) or **biologic** injections (omalizumab, dupilumab). Good inhaler technique—often with a **spacer**—makes a large difference.',
label('resp','Where respiratory drugs act',[['bronchi','Bronchi','Albuterol relaxes smooth muscle here.'],['bronchioles','Bronchioles','Inflamed and narrowed in asthma.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Damaged in emphysema (COPD).'],['trachea','Trachea','Carries inhaled medicine toward the lungs.'],['nasal','Nasal cavity','Intranasal steroids treat allergic rhinitis.']]),
sort('Rescue or controller?',['Quick-relief (rescue)','Long-term controller'],[['Albuterol',0],['Fluticasone',1],['Montelukast',1],['Levalbuterol',0],['Budesonide',1],['Fluticasone/salmeterol',1]])]],
['COPD and other respiratory treatments',[
'**Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)**, including **emphysema** and **chronic bronchitis**, is usually caused by smoking and causes long-term airflow limitation. Treatment relies on **long-acting bronchodilators**: **LAMA** (long-acting muscarinic antagonists) such as **tiotropium** (Spiriva) and **umeclidinium**, and LABAs, sometimes combined with an ICS. Short-acting **ipratropium** and albuterol are used for relief. **Smoking cessation** is the most important treatment; options include **nicotine replacement** (patch, gum, lozenge), **varenicline** and **bupropion**.',
'Other respiratory drugs include **antitussives** (dextromethorphan; codeine-containing cough syrups are controlled), **expectorants** (guaifenesin) that thin mucus, and **decongestants** such as pseudoephedrine, which is kept **behind the counter** with purchase limits because it can be used to make methamphetamine. Oxygen is a drug too—it must be prescribed and kept away from open flames.',
match('COPD and respiratory drugs',[['Tiotropium','Once-daily LAMA for COPD'],['Ipratropium','Short-acting anticholinergic bronchodilator'],['Varenicline','Smoking-cessation medicine'],['Guaifenesin','Expectorant that thins mucus'],['Dextromethorphan','Cough suppressant'],['Pseudoephedrine','Decongestant sold behind the counter']])]],
['Counseling points to refer',[
'Technicians do not counsel, but recognizing important issues helps them alert the pharmacist. Examples: a patient on lisinopril who reports **facial or tongue swelling** (angioedema—an emergency); a patient picking up warfarin who mentions a **new antibiotic**; someone who says they **stopped their beta blocker suddenly**; a patient using their **albuterol inhaler every day** (asthma may be poorly controlled); or a patient asking whether they can take **ibuprofen** with their blood thinner.',
"Technicians also help with **adherence**. Many cardiovascular and respiratory medicines treat conditions that cause no symptoms day to day, so patients may skip doses. Tools include **synchronized refills** so all medicines are due on the same day, blister or pill-box packaging, refill reminders and 90-day supplies when the plan allows. Late refills on a blood pressure, cholesterol or controller-inhaler prescription are a signal to run the late-refill screening from Skill V and refer the patient to the pharmacist.",
kase('At the pickup window',{a:{text:'A patient picking up warfarin says, “My dentist also gave me an antibiotic and told me to take ibuprofen.” What should you do?',choices:[['Alert the pharmacist before completing the sale','b','Correct. Both may increase bleeding risk with warfarin.',true],['Ring it up—it is not your job','b','Technicians flag potential interactions for the pharmacist.',false]]},b:{text:'Another patient says they use their albuterol rescue inhaler four times every day. What does that suggest?',choices:[['Asthma may be poorly controlled; refer to the pharmacist','c','Correct. Frequent rescue use needs evaluation.',true],['That is normal use','c','Rescue inhalers should not be needed daily for well-controlled asthma.',false]]},c:{text:'The pharmacist counsels both patients and contacts the prescribers. Good catches.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Hypertension: thiazides, -pril (cough, angioedema), -sartan, -dipine (edema), -olol (do not stop suddenly); statins lower LDL (muscle pain); SL nitroglycerin for angina.','Heart failure: loop diuretics (low K⁺), ACE/ARB/ARNI, beta blockers, spironolactone, SGLT2 inhibitors, digoxin (narrow index); warfarin (INR, vitamin K), DOACs and antiplatelets raise bleeding risk.','Asthma: SABA rescue (albuterol) plus controllers (ICS—rinse mouth; montelukast; LABA only with ICS); COPD: LAMA/LABA, smoking cessation; pseudoephedrine is behind the counter.'],terms:[['Hypertension','Blood pressure consistently at or above 130/80 mm Hg.'],['Anticoagulant','A drug that slows blood clot formation.'],['Bronchodilator','A drug that opens the airways.']],mcqs:[q('Which drug class commonly causes a dry cough?','ACE inhibitors','ARBs','Statins','Thiazides','Switching to an ARB often helps.'),q('Losartan belongs to which class?','ARB','ACE inhibitor','Beta blocker','Calcium channel blocker','-sartan stem.'),q('Which drug requires INR monitoring?','Warfarin','Apixaban','Atorvastatin','Albuterol','Vitamin K affects it.'),q('Which is a rescue inhaler?','Albuterol','Fluticasone','Tiotropium','Montelukast','SABA, works in minutes.'),q('Patients should rinse their mouth after using:','An inhaled corticosteroid','Albuterol','Nitroglycerin','A nicotine patch','Prevents oral thrush.'),q('Furosemide commonly causes:','Low potassium','High potassium','Dry cough','Weight gain from fluid','Loop diuretic.'),q('Which drug is used once daily for COPD maintenance?','Tiotropium','Albuterol','Guaifenesin','Pseudoephedrine','A LAMA.'),q('Amlodipine’s common side effect is:','Ankle swelling','Dry cough','Hair loss','Low blood sugar','Calcium channel blocker.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient’s profile lists lisinopril, metoprolol succinate ER, atorvastatin, apixaban and albuterol HFA. Identify the class and main purpose of each and name one safety concern for each that a technician should be alert to and refer to the pharmacist.',rubric:[['Cardiovascular classes',['ace inhibitor','beta blocker','statin','anticoagulant']],['Purposes',['blood pressure','heart rate','cholesterol','clot','atrial fibrillation']],['Respiratory',['albuterol','rescue','short-acting','bronchodilator','asthma']],['Safety concerns',['cough','angioedema','stop suddenly','crush','muscle pain','bleeding','daily use','refer']]],model:'Lisinopril is an ACE inhibitor used for high blood pressure and heart protection; a dry cough or, more seriously, swelling of the lips, face or tongue (angioedema) should be referred to the pharmacist immediately. Metoprolol succinate ER is a beta blocker that lowers heart rate and blood pressure; it should not be stopped suddenly, and because it is extended-release it should not be crushed, so a patient asking about stopping or crushing it should be referred. Atorvastatin is a statin that lowers LDL cholesterol; unexplained muscle pain or weakness should be reported. Apixaban is a direct oral anticoagulant that prevents clots, for example in atrial fibrillation; it increases bleeding risk, so signs of bleeding or questions about OTC NSAIDs such as ibuprofen go to the pharmacist. Albuterol HFA is a short-acting beta₂-agonist rescue inhaler for asthma or bronchospasm; if the patient uses it daily or more often, asthma may be poorly controlled and the pharmacist should be told. Technicians recognize and refer these concerns rather than counseling.'},sources:[RULES,OSP_CV,AHA_HBP,OSP_RESP,NHLBI_ASTHMA]};

export const R9={title:'Disorders and treatments II: diabetes, thyroid, digestive and infectious diseases',hook:'About one in nine Americans has diabetes, and antibiotics are among the most-prescribed drugs in the world. Some of the most important pharmacy safety lessons live here: insulin is one of the highest-alert medications, and antibiotic overuse drives resistant “superbugs.” This lesson covers endocrine, digestive and infectious disorders and their treatments.',objectives:['Compare type 1 and type 2 diabetes and the drug classes used for each.','Describe insulin types, storage and safety.','Identify treatments for thyroid and common digestive disorders.','Match antibiotic, antiviral and antifungal classes to examples and key counseling points.'],sections:[
['Diabetes and insulin',[
'**Diabetes mellitus** is high blood glucose from too little insulin or poor response to it. **Type 1** is an autoimmune loss of insulin-producing beta cells and always requires **insulin**. **Type 2** involves **insulin resistance** and is treated with lifestyle changes, oral and injectable medicines and sometimes insulin. Symptoms of high glucose include thirst, frequent urination and blurred vision; **hypoglycemia** (low glucose) causes shakiness, sweating, confusion and hunger and is treated with fast-acting sugar.',
'Insulin types: **rapid-acting** (lispro/Humalog, aspart/NovoLog) is taken with meals; **short-acting** (regular, Humulin R/Novolin R); **intermediate** (**NPH**, cloudy—roll gently to mix); **long-acting** (glargine/Lantus, detemir, degludec) provides baseline insulin. Most insulin is **U-100** (100 units/mL) and measured in an **insulin syringe** marked in units; never abbreviate “units” as **U**. Unopened insulin is **refrigerated**; in-use vials and pens can usually stay at room temperature for a set number of days (often 28) depending on the product—never freeze insulin.',
{t:'w',kind:'glucose',title:'Glucose and insulin',prompt:'Compare a healthy body with type 1 and type 2 diabetes after a meal. Which situation needs insulin from outside the body?'},
sort('Insulin type',['Rapid-acting','Long-acting'],[['Insulin lispro',0],['Insulin glargine',1],['Insulin aspart',0],['Insulin degludec',1],['Insulin detemir',1]])]],
['Type 2 diabetes medicines',[
'**Metformin** (Glucophage) is usually first-line; it lowers liver glucose production and can cause **stomach upset** (taking it with meals helps) and, rarely, lactic acidosis. **Sulfonylureas** such as **glipizide** and **glimepiride** make the pancreas release more insulin and can cause **hypoglycemia**. **DPP-4 inhibitors** (**-gliptin**: sitagliptin) and **GLP-1 receptor agonists** (**-glutide**: semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide; tirzepatide is a dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist) increase insulin after meals; GLP-1 drugs often cause nausea and weight loss.',
'**SGLT2 inhibitors** (**-gliflozin**: empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) make the kidneys remove glucose in the urine and protect the heart and kidneys; they can cause genital yeast infections and dehydration. **Thiazolidinediones** (**-glitazone**: pioglitazone) increase insulin sensitivity but can cause fluid retention. Patients with diabetes also often take statins, ACE inhibitors or ARBs to protect the heart and kidneys.',
match('Match the stem or drug',[['Metformin','First-line biguanide; take with meals'],['Glipizide','Sulfonylurea; can cause low blood sugar'],['-gliptin','DPP-4 inhibitor'],['-glutide','GLP-1 receptor agonist'],['-gliflozin','SGLT2 inhibitor'],['-glitazone','Thiazolidinedione']])]],
['Thyroid and other endocrine disorders',[
'**Hypothyroidism** (too little thyroid hormone) causes fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance and constipation. It is treated with **levothyroxine** (Synthroid, Levoxyl), taken on an **empty stomach 30–60 minutes before breakfast** and separated from **calcium, iron and antacids** by about four hours. Brands and generics may not be interchanged freely without the prescriber’s knowledge in some practices because small dose differences matter. **Hyperthyroidism** (too much hormone) causes weight loss, heat intolerance and a rapid heartbeat and may be treated with **methimazole** or **propylthiouracil**, radioactive iodine or surgery.',
'**Corticosteroids** such as **prednisone** and **methylprednisolone** treat inflammation and adrenal insufficiency; they raise blood glucose, can upset the stomach and should not be stopped suddenly after long use. **Osteoporosis** is treated with **bisphosphonates** (alendronate: take with a full glass of water and stay upright 30 minutes), plus calcium and vitamin D.',
label('endocrine','Endocrine glands and their drugs',[['thyroid','Thyroid','Levothyroxine replaces its hormone.'],['pancreas','Pancreas','Beta cells make insulin; damaged in type 1 diabetes.'],['adrenal','Adrenal glands','Make cortisol; prednisone mimics it.'],['pituitary','Pituitary','The “master gland” that signals other glands.'],['parathyroid','Parathyroid','Regulates calcium.']]),
chk('How should levothyroxine usually be taken?',['On an empty stomach 30–60 minutes before breakfast','With a calcium supplement at dinner','Only when tired','With antacids'],'Food, calcium and iron reduce absorption.')]],
['Digestive disorders',[
'**Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)** and **peptic ulcers** are treated with **proton pump inhibitors** (**-prazole**: omeprazole/Prilosec, pantoprazole/Protonix, esomeprazole/Nexium), usually taken **30–60 minutes before a meal**; **H₂ blockers** (**-tidine**: famotidine/Pepcid); and **antacids** (calcium carbonate, magnesium hydroxide) for quick relief. Ulcers caused by ***H. pylori*** bacteria need antibiotics plus a PPI.',
'**Constipation** may be treated with fiber (psyllium), stool softeners (**docusate**), stimulant laxatives (**senna**, bisacodyl) and osmotic laxatives (**polyethylene glycol**/MiraLAX). **Diarrhea** may be treated with **loperamide** (Imodium) or bismuth subsalicylate (which can darken stools and should be avoided in children with viral illness because of its salicylate). **Nausea** is treated with **ondansetron** (Zofran), promethazine or meclizine for motion sickness. **Opioids** commonly cause constipation, so a laxative is often prescribed with them.',
sort('Which digestive problem does it treat?',['Acid / GERD','Constipation','Diarrhea','Nausea'],[['Omeprazole',0],['Famotidine',0],['Docusate',1],['Polyethylene glycol',1],['Loperamide',2],['Ondansetron',3]])]],
['Antibiotics',[
'**Antibiotics** treat **bacterial** infections; they do not work against viruses such as colds or flu. **Penicillins** (**-cillin**: amoxicillin, amoxicillin/clavulanate/Augmentin) are common for ear and throat infections; penicillin **allergy** is the most commonly reported drug allergy. **Cephalosporins** (**cef-/ceph-**: cephalexin/Keflex, ceftriaxone) have a small cross-reactivity with penicillin allergy. **Macrolides** (**-mycin**: azithromycin/Zithromax, clarithromycin) treat respiratory infections. **Fluoroquinolones** (**-floxacin**: ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) carry boxed warnings about **tendon rupture** and are separated from **antacids, dairy, calcium and iron**.',
'**Tetracyclines** (**-cycline**: doxycycline) cause **sun sensitivity** and are generally avoided in young children and pregnancy; they also bind dairy and minerals. **Sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim** (Bactrim) requires plenty of water and can cause sun sensitivity and rash. **Metronidazole** (Flagyl) causes a severe reaction with **alcohol**. **Nitrofurantoin** treats urinary tract infections; **vancomycin** treats serious infections such as MRSA and *C. difficile* (orally). Patients should take antibiotics **exactly as prescribed** and report rash, hives or severe diarrhea.',
match('Antibiotic counseling flags',[['Amoxicillin','Penicillin—check allergy; shake suspension'],['Ciprofloxacin','Tendon warning; separate from antacids and dairy'],['Doxycycline','Sun sensitivity; avoid dairy at dosing time'],['Metronidazole','No alcohol'],['Azithromycin','Macrolide; often a 5-day pack'],['Sulfamethoxazole/trimethoprim','Drink plenty of water; sun sensitivity']])]],
['Antivirals, antifungals and stewardship',[
'**Antivirals** target specific viruses: **oseltamivir** (Tamiflu) for influenza works best when started **within 48 hours** of symptoms; **acyclovir** and **valacyclovir** treat herpes and shingles; **nirmatrelvir/ritonavir** (Paxlovid) treats COVID-19 and has many interactions; HIV is treated with combination **antiretroviral therapy**. **Antifungals** include **fluconazole** (**-azole**), **nystatin** for oral thrush and topical **clotrimazole** and **terbinafine**.',
'**Antibiotic resistance** happens when bacteria survive and multiply after exposure to antibiotics. The CDC estimates that at least 2.8 million antibiotic-resistant infections occur in the U.S. each year. **Antibiotic stewardship** means using antibiotics only when needed, choosing the right drug, dose and duration and never sharing or saving leftover antibiotics. Pharmacy staff support stewardship by explaining that antibiotics do not treat colds and by encouraging patients to follow directions exactly.',
kase('Antibiotic questions',{a:{text:'A parent asks for “some leftover amoxicillin” from last year for a child’s cold. What should you do?',choices:[['Explain that you cannot dispense it without a new prescription and refer them to the pharmacist','b','Correct. Antibiotics do not treat colds, and old supplies are unsafe.',true],['Give them what is left','b','That is illegal and unsafe.',false]]},b:{text:'A patient picking up ciprofloxacin mentions taking calcium supplements every morning. What now?',choices:[['Alert the pharmacist to counsel on separating the doses','c','Correct. Calcium binds ciprofloxacin and lowers absorption.',true],['Say nothing','c','This is an important interaction to flag.',false]]},c:{text:'The pharmacist counsels the patient on timing. Stewardship and safety in action.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Type 1 diabetes needs insulin (rapid, short, NPH, long-acting; U-100, write “units”, refrigerate unopened); type 2 uses metformin, sulfonylureas, -gliptins, -glutides, -gliflozins and sometimes insulin.','Levothyroxine on an empty stomach; PPIs (-prazole) before meals, H₂ blockers (-tidine); laxatives, loperamide and ondansetron for other GI problems.','Antibiotics: -cillin (allergy), cephalosporins, -mycin, -floxacin (tendons, antacids), -cycline (sun, dairy), metronidazole (no alcohol); antivirals are virus-specific; practice stewardship.'],terms:[['Hypoglycemia','Low blood glucose that causes shakiness, sweating and confusion.'],['Proton pump inhibitor','A drug that strongly reduces stomach acid, such as omeprazole.'],['Antibiotic stewardship','Using antibiotics only when needed, in the right dose and duration.']],mcqs:[q('Which diabetes type always requires insulin?','Type 1','Type 2','Gestational only','Prediabetes','Beta cells are destroyed.'),q('Insulin glargine is:','Long-acting','Rapid-acting','Short-acting','An oral tablet','Provides basal insulin.'),q('Which drug is usually first-line for type 2 diabetes?','Metformin','Insulin glargine','Glipizide','Levothyroxine','Take with meals.'),q('How should levothyroxine be taken?','On an empty stomach before breakfast','With milk at bedtime','With antacids','Only when symptoms occur','Food and minerals reduce absorption.'),q('Omeprazole belongs to which class?','Proton pump inhibitor','H₂ blocker','Antacid','Laxative','-prazole stem.'),q('Which antibiotic should not be taken with alcohol?','Metronidazole','Amoxicillin','Azithromycin','Cephalexin','Severe reaction.'),q('Which antibiotic class has a boxed warning about tendon rupture?','Fluoroquinolones','Penicillins','Macrolides','Cephalosporins','Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin.'),q('Oseltamivir treats:','Influenza','Bacterial pneumonia','Herpes','Fungal infections','Best within 48 hours.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare the treatment of type 1 and type 2 diabetes, including at least four drug classes, and explain two insulin safety practices pharmacy technicians must follow.',rubric:[['Disease difference',['autoimmune','beta cells','insulin resistance','type 1','type 2']],['Insulin types',['rapid','lispro','long','glargine','nph','basal']],['Type 2 classes',['metformin','sulfonylurea','glipizide','gliptin','glutide','gliflozin','sglt2','glp-1']],['Insulin safety',['u-100','units','insulin syringe','refrigerate','freeze','high-alert','hypoglycemia']]],model:'Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune loss of the pancreatic beta cells, so the body makes little or no insulin and every patient needs insulin: usually a long-acting basal insulin such as glargine plus a rapid-acting insulin such as lispro with meals. Type 2 diabetes is insulin resistance with declining insulin release, so treatment starts with lifestyle changes and metformin, which lowers liver glucose output and is taken with meals. Other classes include sulfonylureas such as glipizide, which increase insulin release but can cause hypoglycemia; DPP-4 inhibitors (-gliptin); GLP-1 receptor agonists (-glutide) such as semaglutide, which increase insulin after meals and cause weight loss; and SGLT2 inhibitors (-gliflozin), which remove glucose in the urine and protect the heart and kidneys. Some people with type 2 diabetes eventually need insulin. Insulin is a high-alert medication. Technicians must always write “units” rather than the dangerous abbreviation “U,” match U-100 insulin with a U-100 insulin syringe, double-check the insulin type because names such as Humalog and Humulin look alike, and store unopened insulin in the refrigerator without freezing it, following each product’s room-temperature limits once in use.'},sources:[RULES,NIDDK_DM,OSP_ENDO,CDC_ABX]};

export const R10={title:'Disorders and treatments III: nervous system, mental health, pain, allergy and vaccines',hook:'Depression, anxiety, ADHD, seizures, chronic pain and allergies affect millions of people—and many of the drugs that treat them are controlled substances, have narrow safety margins or need careful counseling. Vaccines prevent disease rather than treating it, and pharmacies now give a large share of them. This lesson completes the disorders and treatments content and links back to the vaccine screening checklists in Skill V.',objectives:['Match drugs for depression, anxiety, ADHD, sleep, psychosis and bipolar disorder to their classes and key cautions.','Identify treatments for seizures, Parkinson’s disease, Alzheimer’s disease and migraine.','Compare acetaminophen, NSAIDs and opioids, including safety limits and naloxone.','Describe allergy treatments, anaphylaxis, vaccine types and vaccine storage.'],sections:[
['Mental health medicines',[
'**Depression** is often treated with **SSRIs** (sertraline/Zoloft, fluoxetine/Prozac, escitalopram/Lexapro, citalopram, paroxetine) or **SNRIs** (venlafaxine, duloxetine/Cymbalta). Other options include **bupropion** (Wellbutrin) and mirtazapine. These medicines take **2–6 weeks** for full effect, should not be stopped abruptly, and carry an FDA **boxed warning** about suicidal thoughts in children and young adults. Combining serotonergic drugs (or with St. John’s wort or some migraine drugs) can cause **serotonin syndrome**.',
'**Anxiety** may be treated with SSRIs, **buspirone** or short-term **benzodiazepines** (alprazolam/Xanax, lorazepam/Ativan, clonazepam—**Schedule IV**, sedating, dangerous with alcohol or opioids). **ADHD** is treated with stimulants such as **methylphenidate** (Ritalin, Concerta) and **amphetamine salts** (Adderall)—**Schedule II**—or non-stimulants such as atomoxetine. **Insomnia** may be treated with **zolpidem** (Ambien, C-IV). **Schizophrenia** and bipolar disorder use **antipsychotics** (risperidone, quetiapine, aripiprazole, olanzapine), which can cause weight gain and movement disorders. **Lithium** for bipolar disorder has a **narrow therapeutic index**: blood levels are monitored, and dehydration or NSAIDs can raise levels.',
sort('Drug class',['SSRI','Benzodiazepine','Stimulant','Antipsychotic'],[['Sertraline',0],['Escitalopram',0],['Alprazolam',1],['Lorazepam',1],['Methylphenidate',2],['Amphetamine salts',2],['Risperidone',3],['Aripiprazole',3]])]],
['Neurologic disorders',[
'**Epilepsy** (recurrent seizures) is treated with antiseizure medicines such as **levetiracetam** (Keppra), **lamotrigine** (Lamictal—report any **rash**, which can be serious), **carbamazepine**, **valproic acid** and **phenytoin** (Dilantin—narrow therapeutic index, gum overgrowth). Patients should never stop these suddenly. **Parkinson’s disease** results from loss of dopamine-producing neurons and is treated with **carbidopa/levodopa** (Sinemet), dopamine agonists (pramipexole, ropinirole) and MAO-B inhibitors.',
'**Alzheimer’s disease** is treated with cholinesterase inhibitors such as **donepezil** (Aricept) and with **memantine**; newer antibody drugs target amyloid plaques. **Migraine** may be treated at onset with **triptans** (**-triptan**: sumatriptan/Imitrex, rizatriptan) or NSAIDs and prevented with beta blockers, topiramate or CGRP-targeting drugs. **Gabapentin** and **pregabalin** (C-V) treat nerve pain and seizures. **Muscle relaxants** such as cyclobenzaprine cause drowsiness.',
match('Neurologic treatments',[['Levetiracetam','Seizures'],['Carbidopa/levodopa','Parkinson’s disease'],['Donepezil','Alzheimer’s disease'],['Sumatriptan','Migraine attack'],['Lamotrigine','Seizures and bipolar disorder; report rash'],['Gabapentin','Nerve pain and seizures']])]],
['Pain management',[
'**Acetaminophen** (Tylenol, APAP) relieves pain and fever but not inflammation. It is safe at proper doses but can cause **liver damage** in overdose; the maximum for most healthy adults is **4 g per day**, and many labels recommend less. It is hidden in many combination products (hydrocodone/APAP, cold medicines), so patients may take too much without knowing. **NSAIDs** (ibuprofen/Motrin/Advil, naproxen/Aleve, meloxicam, celecoxib) relieve pain, fever and inflammation but can cause **stomach bleeding**, kidney problems and higher blood pressure; take with food and avoid with anticoagulants unless the prescriber approves.',
'**Opioids** (hydrocodone/APAP/Norco, oxycodone/OxyContin, morphine, hydromorphone, fentanyl patches, tramadol) treat moderate to severe pain. They cause **constipation, sedation, nausea** and, in overdose, **respiratory depression**. Most are **Schedule II** (tramadol is C-IV). They must not be combined with alcohol or benzodiazepines without medical direction. **Naloxone** (Narcan) reverses opioid overdose and is available without a prescription as a nasal spray. **Gout** is treated with **colchicine** and NSAIDs for flares and **allopurinol** for prevention.',
{t:'example',title:'Hidden acetaminophen',prompt:'A patient takes hydrocodone/APAP 5/325 mg, 2 tablets every 6 hours, and wants to add Extra Strength Tylenol 500 mg, 2 tablets every 6 hours. (Invented for practice.)',steps:['Prescription APAP: 325 mg × 2 tablets × 4 doses = 2,600 mg/day','OTC APAP: 500 mg × 2 × 4 = 4,000 mg/day','Total: 6,600 mg/day'],answer:'That exceeds 4 g/day—refer to the pharmacist immediately.'},
sort('Analgesic type',['Acetaminophen','NSAID','Opioid'],[['Tylenol',0],['Ibuprofen',1],['Naproxen',1],['Oxycodone',2],['Celecoxib',1],['Morphine',2]])]],
['Allergies and anaphylaxis',[
'**Allergic rhinitis** (hay fever) causes sneezing, a runny or stuffy nose and itchy eyes. Treatments include **second-generation antihistamines** (cetirizine/Zyrtec, loratadine/Claritin, fexofenadine/Allegra), which cause less drowsiness than **first-generation** diphenhydramine (Benadryl) and hydroxyzine; **intranasal corticosteroids** (fluticasone/Flonase), which work best when used daily; decongestants; and montelukast. Allergen immunotherapy (allergy shots or tablets) can reduce sensitivity over time.',
'**Anaphylaxis** is a severe, life-threatening allergic reaction: hives, swelling of the face or throat, wheezing, vomiting and a drop in blood pressure, often within minutes of exposure to foods, insect stings or drugs. **Epinephrine** is the first-line treatment, given by **auto-injector** (EpiPen, Auvi-Q) into the **outer thigh**, followed by calling 911. Patients at risk carry two auto-injectors, check expiration dates and keep them at room temperature.',
match('Allergy treatments',[['Cetirizine','Second-generation antihistamine'],['Diphenhydramine','Sedating first-generation antihistamine'],['Fluticasone nasal spray','Intranasal corticosteroid'],['Epinephrine auto-injector','First-line for anaphylaxis'],['Pseudoephedrine','Oral decongestant'],['Montelukast','Leukotriene modifier']]),
chk('Where is an epinephrine auto-injector given?',['Outer thigh','Into a vein','In the arm only','Under the tongue'],'It can be given through clothing; call 911 afterward.')]],
['Vaccines and screening',[
'Vaccines train the immune system to recognize a germ. **Inactivated** vaccines (such as **IPV** polio, influenza shots and hepatitis B) contain killed germs or pieces and cannot cause the disease. **Live attenuated** vaccines (such as **MMR**—measles, mumps and **rubella**—varicella and the nasal flu vaccine) contain weakened germs and are generally **not given during pregnancy** or to people with **weakened immune systems**. This is why the rubella screening asks about immunosuppression, corticosteroids, tuberculosis and pregnancy, and why the IPV screening asks about immune deficiency.',
'Vaccines must stay in the **cold chain**: most are refrigerated at **2–8 °C (36–46 °F)**, and some (such as varicella) are frozen. Temperatures are monitored with digital data loggers, and vaccines exposed to the wrong temperature are labeled “Do not use” and reported. Patients receive a **Vaccine Information Statement (VIS)** before vaccination, and every dose is documented and often reported to a state immunization registry. Pharmacists in all states can administer many vaccines, with technicians assisting and, in many states, administering under specific training.',
sort('Live or inactivated?',['Live attenuated','Inactivated or non-live'],[['MMR (rubella)',0],['IPV (polio)',1],['Varicella',0],['Influenza injection',1],['Hepatitis B',1],['Nasal influenza vaccine',0]])]],
['Putting it together',[
'The disorders and treatments section rewards pattern recognition. When you see a new drug name, look for a **stem**, connect it to a **class**, and recall the **disorder**, a **key side effect** and a **counseling flag**. Build flashcards for the top 200 drugs with brand, generic, class, schedule if controlled and one safety point.',
flip('Stem review',[['-triptan','Migraine (serotonin agonist)'],['-pam / -lam','Benzodiazepine'],['-oxetine / -aline','Antidepressants (SSRIs/SNRIs)'],['-profen','NSAID'],['-tidine','H₂ blocker'],['-mab','Monoclonal antibody'],['-vir','Antiviral'],['-azole','Antifungal (or PPI if -prazole)']]),
kase('High-alert situations',{a:{text:'A teenager picking up a new sertraline prescription asks if it will work today for an exam. How should this be handled?',choices:[['Refer to the pharmacist, who explains it takes weeks and discusses warnings','b','Correct. Counseling is the pharmacist’s role.',true],['Say it works instantly','b','SSRIs take weeks to reach full effect.',false]]},b:{text:'A patient buying naloxone asks if they need a prescription. What is true?',choices:[['Naloxone nasal spray is available over the counter','c','Correct. Reversing opioid overdose saves lives.',true],['It is a Schedule II drug','c','Naloxone is not a controlled substance.',false]]},c:{text:'The pharmacist completes the counseling. You have now covered all seven areas of the test plan.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['SSRIs/SNRIs take weeks and carry a youth suicidality warning; benzodiazepines and zolpidem are C-IV; ADHD stimulants are C-II; lithium and phenytoin have narrow therapeutic indexes.','Acetaminophen max about 4 g/day (watch combinations); NSAIDs cause GI bleeding; opioids cause constipation and respiratory depression—naloxone reverses overdose.','Second-generation antihistamines, intranasal steroids and epinephrine for anaphylaxis; live vaccines (MMR) avoid pregnancy and immunosuppression; store most vaccines at 2–8 °C.'],terms:[['Narrow therapeutic index','A small difference between an effective and a toxic dose.'],['Anaphylaxis','A severe, life-threatening allergic reaction.'],['Live attenuated vaccine','A vaccine containing a weakened form of the germ.']],mcqs:[q('Sertraline belongs to which class?','SSRI','Benzodiazepine','Stimulant','Antipsychotic','Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor.'),q('Methylphenidate is in which DEA schedule?','Schedule II','Schedule IV','Schedule V','Not controlled','ADHD stimulant.'),q('What is the usual maximum daily acetaminophen dose for healthy adults?','4 g','10 g','1 g','400 mg','Many labels recommend less.'),q('Which drug reverses opioid overdose?','Naloxone','Naproxen','Lorazepam','Ondansetron','Available OTC as a nasal spray.'),q('Which antihistamine causes the most drowsiness?','Diphenhydramine','Loratadine','Fexofenadine','Cetirizine','First-generation.'),q('Carbidopa/levodopa treats:','Parkinson’s disease','Epilepsy','Alzheimer’s disease','Migraine','Replaces dopamine.'),q('Which vaccine is live attenuated?','MMR','IPV','Hepatitis B','Influenza injection','Avoid in pregnancy.'),q('Most refrigerated vaccines are stored at:','2–8 °C (36–46 °F)','−20 °C only','Room temperature','15–25 °C','The cold chain.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient has prescriptions for hydrocodone/acetaminophen, alprazolam and sertraline and asks about adding OTC Tylenol PM (acetaminophen and diphenhydramine) for sleep. Identify each drug’s class and schedule (if controlled), explain the safety concerns with this combination and describe what the technician should do.',rubric:[['Classes and schedules',['opioid','schedule ii','benzodiazepine','schedule iv','ssri']],['Acetaminophen concern',['acetaminophen','4 g','liver','combination']],['Sedation concerns',['sedation','respiratory depression','diphenhydramine','drowsiness','alcohol']],['Technician action',['refer','pharmacist','do not counsel','profile','naloxone']]],model:'Hydrocodone/acetaminophen is an opioid analgesic combination in Schedule II; alprazolam is a benzodiazepine in Schedule IV; and sertraline is an SSRI antidepressant that is not controlled. Tylenol PM contains acetaminophen plus diphenhydramine, a sedating first-generation antihistamine. The first concern is duplicate acetaminophen: the patient already gets acetaminophen in the hydrocodone combination, and adding more could exceed the usual 4 g daily maximum and cause liver damage. The second concern is additive sedation: an opioid, a benzodiazepine and diphenhydramine all depress the central nervous system, and together they increase the risk of excessive sleepiness, falls and dangerous respiratory depression, especially with alcohol. Diphenhydramine can also cause confusion in older adults. The technician should not give advice; they should alert the pharmacist before the sale, make sure the patient profile lists all prescription and OTC products, and let the pharmacist counsel the patient, who may also recommend having naloxone on hand.'},sources:[RULES,NIMH_MEDS,FDA_OPIOID,AAAAI_RHIN,CDC_STORAGE]};
